Polymorphism = One thing, many forms

It allows the same method name to behave differently depending on the object.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Types of Polymorphism

    1. Compile-Time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)

Multiple methods have the same name but different parameters.
Decided by the compiler at compile time.

class Calculator{
    int add(int a, int b){
        return a + b;
    }
    int add(int a, int b, int c){
        return a + b + c;
    }
}

    2. Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)

A subclass provides its own implementation of a parent class method.
Decided at runtime based on the actual object.

class Animal{
    void makeSound(){
        System.out.println("Animal Sound");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal{
    @Override
    void makeSound(){
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}
Animal a = new Dog();
a.makeSound(); // Bark

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    ==>> Constructor Chaining

Constructor Chaining = Calling one constructor from another constructor.

-> Within Same Class

class Test{
    Test(){
        this(10);
    }
    Test(int x){
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

-> Between Parent and Child Class

class Parent{
    Parent(){
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}
class Child extends Parent{
    Child(){
        super();
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        Diamond Problem

Occurs when a class inherits from two classes that have a common parent and both contain the same method.
Leads to ambiguity about which method should be inherited.
      A
     / \
    B   C
     \ /
      D
Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes, so the diamond problem is avoided.
With interfaces, Java resolves it using method overriding.
